@startuml ota-frame
' The UART flash protocol envelope: how one received byte is handled.
' Grounded in Src/ota_frame.c (ota_frame_parser_feed, process, seek_start)
' and in industrial-hmi docs/protocols/uart-flash-v1.md, sections 3 and 9 item 8.

title One byte in: the frame parser and the resync rule

skinparam shadowing false

start
:byte arrives from the UART;
if (candidate empty?) then (yes)
  if (byte == 0xA5?) then (no)
    :drop it\n(telemetry text, the reset 0xFF, noise);
    stop
  endif
endif
:append to the candidate;
repeat
  if (6 bytes held?) then (no)
    :wait for more;
    stop
  endif
  if (LEN > 260?) then (yes)
    #FFE0E0:false start:\nresume from the byte AFTER the 0xA5,\nre-scanning bytes already held;
  else (no)
    if (8 + LEN bytes held?) then (no)
      :wait for the body;
      stop
    endif
    if (CRC-16 of TYPE..PAYLOAD\n== the two bytes after it?) then (yes)
      #E0FFE0:sink(OK);
      :drop the frame,\nskip to the next 0xA5;
    else (no)
      #FFE0E0:sink(BAD_CRC);
      :same resync:\nresume from the byte AFTER the 0xA5;
    endif
  endif
repeat while (bytes left in the candidate?)
stop

note right
  **Never skip LEN bytes.**
  A garbage 0xA5 can carry a garbage LEN
  up to 65535. Waiting for it, or jumping
  past it, swallows the real frames behind.
  Resuming from the next byte finds a real
  frame even inside a false frame's body.

  **A loop, not recursion.** Each false start
  shifts the candidate in place and the loop
  runs again. Recursing would nest up to ~268
  calls, each with its own copy of the bytes.

  **Pinned to the other side.** The host test
  reproduces the spec's CRC check value 0x29B1
  and its two worked frames byte for byte:
  A5 01 01 00 00 00 E9 CD  and  A5 82 01 00 00 00 EB 01.
end note

@enduml
